WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
In our latest Red Team project, we were designing a tool for clinicians to ask diagnostic questions

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.000
and get an answer back from the service.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.000
What we’re looking at here is a start at what that prototype and design could be,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.000
where the clinician asks their initial question and the AI tool provides a highly sourced and relevant response.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
As part of this prototype, we are building an interface around the interface.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
It’s a way to experiment with building the right level of information, transparency and trust for the clinician.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:50.000
On this right-hand panel, we can turn different parts of the UI off and on, to see what’s useful or not in a session with clinicians,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:00.000
as well as see under the hood: what that recommendation algorithm looks like, and why it got to this particular response.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
We also built another panel to explore all the different scenarios of a response the clinician could see,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.000
where you get strong recommendations with high confidence, or low confidence recommendations,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.000
as well as some that are conflicting, presenting a couple of different options for the clinician to make their expert opinion.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:33.000
We built this tool as a way to get quick, high-fidelity feedback from clinicians, exploring lots of variations in real time with the clinician,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:41.000
where we can quickly jump from one response to another, or try turning certain things off and on to see what is useful or not.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:53.500
The whole idea was to figure out the best way to build trust between clinicians and an AI tool, so they can feel confident in the answers they receive.
